   "id": "S3",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Python Lecture Notes",
   "text": "1. Variables and Data Types A variable is a name that refers to a value stored in memory. In Python you create a variable by assignment, for example age = 21. Python is dynamically typed: the type belongs to the value, not to the name, so the same name can later refer to a string. The core built-in types are int (whole numbers), float (decimal numbers), str (text), bool (True or False) and NoneType (the single value None, meaning \"no value\"). The function type(x) reports the type of x. Conversion functions such as int(\"42\"), float(\"3.5\") and str(10) create new values of another type; int(\"abc\") raises a ValueError. Strings are immutable sequences of characters. Indexing starts at zero, so \"python\"[0] is \"p\", and negative indexes count from the end, so \"python\"[-1] is \"n\". Slicing \"python\"[1:4] returns \"yth\" (start included, stop excluded).",
